Apex Outdoor Gear Inc. (an unrelated U.S. importer in Seattle, WA) imports 5,000 carbon-fiber trekking poles from a manufacturer in Taiwan on a CIF Seattle basis. The commercial invoice states a total CIF price of $115,000, which separately itemizes $11,000 for international ocean freight from Kaohsiung to Seattle and $1,500 for marine cargo insurance. In addition to the invoice amount, Apex incurred the following costs not included in the seller's invoice price:
• $4,000 paid to Apex's bona fide buying agent in Taipei for locating the factory and inspecting the goods;
• $8,000 for a specialized aluminum injection mold purchased by Apex from an unrelated U.S. toolmaker and shipped free of charge to the Taiwanese manufacturer for use exclusively on this 5,000-unit order (plus $500 air freight to deliver the mold to the Taiwanese factory);
• $6,000 for industrial design sketches created in Seattle by Apex's full-time U.S.-domiciled staff engineers and emailed free of charge to the Taiwanese factory; and
• $3,500 in patent license royalties that the sales contract requires Apex to pay to a German patent holder as a condition of the sale of the trekking poles for exportation to the United States.

What is the correct transaction value (appraised value) of the imported trekking poles under 19 CFR § 152.103?

Vanguard Medical Devices Inc. (a U.S. importer in Minneapolis, MN) contracts with an unrelated manufacturer in Costa Rica to produce sterile surgical catheters for exportation to the United States. Vanguard provides the following five items free of charge to the Costa Rican factory for use in producing the imported catheters:
1. Specialized biocompatible silicone tubing purchased by Vanguard in Germany for $22,000 and shipped directly to the Costa Rican plant (incorporating into the finished catheters);
2. $15,000 in CAD/CAM engineering blueprints developed in Milan, Italy by an independent Italian engineering firm hired by Vanguard;
3. $18,000 in quality-control testing software architecture written in Minneapolis by Vanguard's own U.S.-domiciled staff engineers;
4. General-purpose air conditioning equipment ($12,000) installed in the Costa Rican factory's administrative accounting office; and
5. $1,200 in international air courier charges paid by Vanguard to transport the German silicone tubing (Item 1) to the Costa Rican factory.

Under 19 CFR § 152.102(a), what is the total dutiable value of the 'assists' that must be added to the price actually paid or payable?
